From 89ec1a6758c3cc153fed8c94174f20cbf809c368 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 01:39:23 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] githooks: force a normalized blob for the commit-msg hook

The eol=lf attribute governs future checkouts and adds; it does not rewrite a blob already
committed, and 'git add --renormalize' saw no change because git compares the working copy
against the blob AFTER normalization - so a stale CRLF object looked identical to a clean LF
working copy. The stored object kept 58 CR bytes while every local check looked correct.

A real line of content forces git to write a fresh blob under the current attributes. The added
line is a note in the file itself saying the endings are load-bearing, so the next person reads
it where they would break it.

Origin: ArifiLabs lane-110D, public-release bar
Measured-effect: packaging only, no engine behaviour changed. Verified against the RAW object
  via git cat-file - git show applies the checkout filter and reported the opposite of the truth.
---
 .githooks/commit-msg | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/.githooks/commit-msg b/.githooks/commit-msg
index e6cbb9a06..015b0bff7 100644
--- a/.githooks/commit-msg
+++ b/.githooks/commit-msg
@@ -1,4 +1,5 @@
 #!/bin/sh
+# Line endings: this file MUST be LF (.gitattributes pins it); CRLF breaks /bin/sh.
 # Reject a commit whose provenance trailers are present but not machine-readable.
 #
 # git ends a trailer block at the first line that is not trailer-shaped, so an unindented
